      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've correctly identified the central moral and political hurdle: the most vulnerable groups, often those who lack a viable alternative to driving, depend most heavily on the streets and carry the highest restraint burden when priced out. To address this \"inequality of price,\" forward-thinking planners argue that the revenue must be used to visibly subsidize the *only* affordable alternative for low-income workers—slashing wait times and fares so that public transit becomes a genuinely cheaper and faster option for everyone.\n\nFurthermore, the \"non-optional\" nature of the policy for climate goals relies on this subsidy being comprehensive enough that no one is truly \"left behind,\" effectively turning the \"regressive tax\" label into a \"progressive investment\" where the costs of the problem (congestion/emissions) are passed to the solvers (the system) and the damages borne by the poor are mitigated.",
